Since prices are positive,

260
00:19:45,135 --> 00:19:46,995
we know this slope is negative.

261
00:19:46,995 --> 00:19:53,395
So if we have an
intercept here like this,

262
00:19:53,395 --> 00:19:55,310
the budget line is going to be

263
00:19:55,310 --> 00:19:58,565
negative and is going
to go somewhere else.

264
00:19:58,565 --> 00:20:01,770
So we know that this slope

265
00:20:02,090 --> 00:20:10,335
equals -p_1/p_2,

266
00:20:10,335 --> 00:20:12,750
and the only thing
that remains to be

267
00:20:12,750 --> 00:20:15,420
found is this intercept.

268
00:20:15,420 --> 00:20:20,790
But that intercept means that
what is the amount I will

269
00:20:20,790 --> 00:20:27,120
purchase of x_1 if
I buy zero of x_2,

270
00:20:27,120 --> 00:20:29,595
which we have actually
solved before.

271
00:20:29,595 --> 00:20:32,370
It is right here.

272
00:20:32,370 --> 00:20:36,225
So we also have that
solution already and that

273
00:20:36,225 --> 00:20:40,185
will be income divided by p_1.

274
00:20:40,185 --> 00:20:42,735
So to wrap up,

275
00:20:42,735 --> 00:20:46,500
we have found three things.

276
00:20:46,500 --> 00:20:50,550
We have found the intercept
in the y axis or x_2,

277
00:20:50,550 --> 00:20:52,770
we have found the slope,

278
00:20:52,770 --> 00:20:57,435
and we have found the
intercept in the x axis,

279
00:20:57,435 --> 00:21:00,430
which is for x_1.

280
00:21:06,350 --> 00:21:09,915
What is also interesting is that

281
00:21:09,915 --> 00:21:19,140
this so called budget line

282
00:21:19,140 --> 00:21:22,935
is dividing the whole
commodity space

283
00:21:22,935 --> 00:21:25,590
in two different regions.

284
00:21:25,590 --> 00:21:31,995
One region is the region
of affordable bundles.

285
00:21:31,995 --> 00:21:35,295
That is all these that I'm
highlighting right now,

286
00:21:35,295 --> 00:21:37,830
all the thing here,

287
00:21:37,830 --> 00:21:41,220
and of course everything
that is inside.

288
00:21:41,220 --> 00:21:45,340
This is what we call
the budget set.

289
00:21:48,290 --> 00:21:53,430
So we can afford all the
bundles that show up here,

290
00:21:53,430 --> 00:21:54,960
all the points that are here,

291
00:21:54,960 --> 00:21:57,615
those are points
that we can afford.

292
00:21:57,615 --> 00:22:03,585
But the same thing is doing
for bundles that are outside.

293
00:22:03,585 --> 00:22:06,735
Bundles that are outside
are not reachable.

294
00:22:06,735 --> 00:22:09,375
We cannot buy these points.

295
00:22:09,375 --> 00:22:14,400
Those will imply expenditure

296
00:22:14,400 --> 00:22:18,700
that is above our means,
above our income.

297
00:22:22,490 --> 00:22:30,100
This here is just the graph
borrowed from our textbook

298
00:22:30,100 --> 00:22:34,950
and it depicts the
same thing we have

299
00:22:34,950 --> 00:22:40,650
been discussing on the
whiteboard or blackboard.

300
00:22:40,650 --> 00:22:43,155
Some of you might be asking,

301
00:22:43,155 --> 00:22:45,840
but what if there are
more than two goods?

302
00:22:45,840 --> 00:22:48,690
Can I still draw that?
And the answer is yes.

303
00:22:48,690 --> 00:22:51,570
You can still draw
the budget set and

304
00:22:51,570 --> 00:22:56,720
the budget surface up
to three commodities,

305
00:22:56,720 --> 00:22:58,520
which is what is depicted here.

306
00:22:58,520 --> 00:23:02,610
The intercepts are the same
as before in the sense

307
00:23:02,610 --> 00:23:07,830
that for x_1 is m/p_1
and for x_2 is m/p_2,

308
00:23:07,830 --> 00:23:11,100
and for x_3 is m/p_3.

309
00:23:11,100 --> 00:23:13,365
However, the question
of course now has

310
00:23:13,365 --> 00:23:17,265
three elements in the
side of the expenditure.

311
00:23:17,265 --> 00:23:20,205
And instead of being
a budget line,

312
00:23:20,205 --> 00:23:25,150
it's now a budget plane
or a budget surface.

313
00:23:25,400 --> 00:23:29,295
Let us do one example.

314
00:23:29,295 --> 00:23:34,110
So suppose income is 100,

315
00:23:34,110 --> 00:23:40,960
p_1 is $3, and p_2= $1.

316
00:23:41,390 --> 00:23:48,825
So let's see how we will
draw this budget line.

317
00:23:48,825 --> 00:23:50,850
First, as always,

318
00:23:50,850 --> 00:23:57,795
we need to start with
drawing the axis x_1, x_2.

319
00:23:57,795 --> 00:24:01,170
And then we need to
find the intercepts.

320
00:24:01,170 --> 00:24:07,200
So one intercept is m/3,

321
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:09,660
which is 100/3 in this case,

322
00:24:09,660 --> 00:24:11,490
income divided by p_1.

323
00:24:11,490 --> 00:24:17,940
And we're going to do
this one somewhere here.

324
00:24:17,940 --> 00:24:22,650
So this is going to be m/p_1.

325
00:24:22,650 --> 00:24:28,470
In this case, this
is equal 200/3.

326
00:24:28,470 --> 00:24:30,750
And in the other case,

327
00:24:30,750 --> 00:24:34,815
we're going to do this up here.

328
00:24:34,815 --> 00:24:44,775
For good 2 it is going
to be m/p_2 = 100/1,

329
00:24:44,775 --> 00:24:47,130
which in this case
is going to be,

330
00:24:47,130 --> 00:24:49,510
of course, just 100.

331
00:24:51,290 --> 00:24:54,195
So the budget line is

332
00:24:54,195 --> 00:24:58,020
the line that connects
these two intercepts.

333
00:24:58,020 --> 00:25:04,515
So 100/3 and simply 100.

334
00:25:04,515 --> 00:25:06,855
And we know more than that.

335
00:25:06,855 --> 00:25:12,855
We know that the slope
is going to be -p_1/p_2,

336
00:25:12,855 --> 00:25:17,920
which in this case is simply -3.
